  "working": "Burning H₂S in O₂ produces SO₂ (sulfur dioxide) as the S-containing product G. SO₂ has a bent shape (approximately 120°) due to two lone pairs on sulfur, giving a molecular shape of bent (or bent with two lone pairs). Reaction of SO₂ with aqueous H₂O₂ (hydrogen peroxide) yields H₂SO₃ (sulfurous acid) as the S-containing product R. H₂SO₃ is tribasic (can donate three protons: two from the S–O bonds and one from the S–H? Actually H₂SO₃ can donate two protons as a diprotic acid; however, in the context of the problem, tribasic likely refers to the ability to form three types of ions upon complete neutralization, typical for sulfite? But common description: sulfurous acid is diprotic. However, many exam contexts call sulfite tribasic because it can react with three equivalents of base? Need evaluate options: (A) S in G is sp³ hybridised – SO₂ uses sp² hybridisation (one lone pair and two bonds), not sp³. (B) R is tribasic – H₂SO₃ can donate two protons, not three; but some may call it tribasic because it can form H₂SO₃ → HSO₃⁻ → SO₃²⁻? Actually sulfite can accept one proton to become bisulfite, and further to sulfate? That would be three basic groups? Might be considered tribasic. (C) R has trigonal pyramidal shape around S – H₂SO₃ has a bent shape similar to water due to two lone pairs, not trigonal pyramidal (which requires three lone pairs or one lone pair with three bonds). (D) Molecular shape around S in G is bent – SO₂ indeed has a bent shape. Therefore only option D is definitely correct. Option B is questionable; typical JEE style would consider sulfite tribasic? Usually sulfite (SO₃²⁻) is monobasic? Actually sulfite can react with acid to give H₂SO₃, which is diprotic. So tribasic likely incorrect. Hence answer D only.",
